---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import CardGrid from '../components/CardGrid.astro';
import { workflowRedirects } from '../data/redirects';
---

<DocsLayout title="Core Workflow" sectionLabel="Development Lifecycle" activeSlug="workflow">
  <section class="section">
    <span class="section-label">Development Lifecycle</span>
    <h2>Core Workflow</h2>
    <p>VibeCrew organizes every project into a structured two-tier workflow. <strong>Tier 1</strong> builds the project foundation (one-time, sequential). <strong>Tier 2</strong> iterates features through a 6-phase cycle. This page provides an overview &mdash; see the sub-pages for details on advanced features, personalization, and best practices.</p>

    <h3>Tier 1: Project Foundation</h3>
    <p>Before any source code can be written, the phase gate enforces six foundation artifacts in sequence:</p>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">/new-project</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">VISION.md</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">design-system.css</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">TDR</span> (Technology Decision Record) &rarr; <span class="highlight">roadmap</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Architecture Diagrams</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">CLAUDE.md</span>
    <span class="muted">Phase gate blocks src/ writes until all six artifacts exist</span></div>

    <p>Each artifact builds on the previous one. The vision defines what you&rsquo;re building, the design system establishes visual language, the TDR locks in technology choices (with optional adversarial review), the roadmap sequences features, the architecture diagrams distill the architecture into compact Mermaid visuals, and the project CLAUDE.md captures conventions for all future sessions.</p>

    <p><strong>Bring Your Own Design System:</strong> If you already have a design system or style guide, you can import it at Step 2 instead of running the Design Discovery interview. VibeCrew accepts CSS files, Tailwind configs, JSON tokens, URLs, Figma links, or brand PDFs. The imported tokens are mapped to VibeCrew&rsquo;s template placeholders, producing identical output files so all downstream agents work unchanged.</p>

    <p>Run <code>/new-project</code> for a new project, or <code>/onboard</code> if you&rsquo;re onboarding an existing codebase. The <a href="index.html">homepage guide</a> walks through either path step by step.</p>

    <h3>Tier 2: Feature Development Cycle</h3>
    <p>Each feature passes through 6 phases. The cycle runs for every feature in your backlog:</p>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">1. Plan</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">2. UI Design</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">3. Code</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">4. Test</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">5. Review</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">6. Docs</span>
    <span class="muted">Each phase produces artifacts tracked by the Vibe Score</span></div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Plan</strong> &mdash; Problem statement, acceptance criteria, component breakdown, API design</li>
      <li><strong>UI Design</strong> &mdash; Component wireframes using design system tokens</li>
      <li><strong>Code</strong> &mdash; Component selection from UI pattern reference, shadcn installation, interaction pattern application, keyboard verification, implementation within TDR boundaries (Builder agent, worktree isolation)</li>
      <li><strong>Test</strong> &mdash; Unit tests, integration tests, optional E2E and accessibility checks</li>
      <li><strong>Review</strong> &mdash; Code review against feature spec, TDR compliance, and conventions</li>
      <li><strong>Docs</strong> &mdash; Feature documentation, CHANGELOG entry, architecture updates if needed</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Key commands">
      <p><code>/new-feature "name"</code> starts a feature. <code>/check</code> runs quality checks mid-session. <code>/wrap</code> finalizes the session with Vibe Score, commit, and session log. <code>/run-backlog</code> automates the full cycle for each feature with <strong>inter-feature context hygiene</strong> &mdash; forced <code>/compact</code> between features to prevent context degradation across long autonomous runs.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Vibe Score</h3>
    <p>Every session ends with a score (0&ndash;100) calculated from real work artifacts. Starts at 100, applies deductions for prompt churn, tool loops, low cache utilization, missing tests, skipped reviews, and documentation drift. Bonuses for clean sessions, high test coverage, TDD discipline, and complete phase artifacts. The Performance Coach proposes permanent CLAUDE.md rule mutations based on patterns it detects.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section">
    <span class="section-label">Deep Dives</span>
    <h2>Explore Workflows</h2>
    <p>The workflow documentation is organized into focused pages:</p>

    <CardGrid>
      <Card href="advanced-features.html" icon="&#9654;" iconColor="purple" title="Advanced Features">
        Workflow templates (<code>/replay</code>), code simplification (<code>/simplify</code>), auto-healing CI (<code>/heal</code>), opponent processor, cost tracking, and multi-language support.
      </Card>

      <Card href="personalization.html" icon="&#9733;" iconColor="green" title="Personalization">
        User profiles (<code>/profile</code>), adaptive agent behavior, and the full gamification system: XP, badges, skill tree, streaks, challenges, and quizzes.
      </Card>

      <Card href="faq-tips.html" icon="&#9888;" iconColor="amber" title="FAQ, Tips &amp; Troubleshooting">
        Frequently asked questions, context window discipline, token efficiency strategies, workflow best practices, common anti-patterns, system review (<code>/system-review</code>), and solutions to frequent issues.
      </Card>
    </CardGrid>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>

<script define:vars={{ redirects: workflowRedirects }}>
(function() {
  var hash = window.location.hash;
  if (hash && redirects[hash]) { window.location.replace(redirects[hash]); }
})();
</script>
